    externalId: 'trajectory:trembley-hydra-regeneration-1744',
    text: 'Between 1740 and 1744 the Genevan naturalist Abraham Trembley demonstrated that the freshwater polyp Hydra, when cut into pieces, regenerates a complete living animal from each fragment — and that polyps cut and grafted could be grown into multi-headed forms. The finding overturned the prevailing assumption that such regeneration belonged to plants rather than animals and that an animal was an indivisible whole, blurring the boundary between the plant and animal kingdoms and challenging contemporary theories of preformation. Trembley was awarded the Royal Society\'s Copley Medal in 1743 and published his results in 1744 as "Mémoires pour servir à l\'histoire d\'un genre de polypes d\'eau douce"; the regenerative capacity of Hydra is now a settled fact and a standard model in regeneration biology.',
