    externalId: 'trajectory:western-schism-disputed-papacy-1378',
    text: 'On 8 April 1378 the cardinals at Rome, under pressure from a crowd demanding a Roman or Italian pope, elected Bartolomeo Prignano as Pope Urban VI. Within months a majority of the same cardinals declared that election invalid as having been made out of fear of the mob (impressio et metus) and, on 20 September 1378, elected Robert of Geneva as a rival pope, Clement VII, who established himself at Avignon. The question of which line held the legitimate papacy — a fact on which the validity of sacraments, appointments, and excommunications across Latin Christendom depended — remained genuinely unsettled for nearly four decades, with Europe divided into competing obediences. The Western Schism was resolved only by the Council of Constance, which secured the abdication of the Roman claimant and the deposition of the rivals and elected Martin V on 11 November 1417.',
