        datePrecision: 'YEAR',
        reason: 'After arriving in Paris in 1778, Mesmer set out his doctrine in the Mémoire sur la découverte du magnétisme animal (1779), claiming that an invisible natural fluid pervaded the universe and that illness arose from obstacles to its flow, which a trained magnétiseur could remove to provoke a healing "crisis." The theory and its group treatments around the baquet became a fashionable sensation among the Parisian public, with paying clients and an organised Society of Harmony spreading the practice across France.',
