    externalId: 'trajectory:lippershey-telescope-patent-1608',
    text: 'On 2 October 1608 the spectacle-maker Hans Lippershey of Middelburg petitioned the States General of the Dutch Republic for a patent on an instrument "for seeing things far away as if they were nearby" — the earliest documented record of the telescope. The States General declined to grant an exclusive patent because rival makers (Jacob Metius of Alkmaar, and reputedly Zacharias Janssen of Middelburg) laid claim to the same device, but rewarded Lippershey for supplying copies. Word of the instrument spread across Europe within months, reaching Galileo Galilei, who built an improved version in 1609 and turned it on the heavens — fixing the telescope as one of the foundational instruments of modern science.',
