        toAxis: 'SETTLED',
        community: 'INSTITUTIONAL',
        occurredAt: '1551-01-01',
        datePrecision: 'YEAR',
        reason: 'When Protestant reformers in the sixteenth century denied transubstantiation, the Council of Trent reaffirmed and dogmatically fixed it: its thirteenth session (1551), in the Decree on the Most Holy Sacrament of the Eucharist, declared that the whole substance of the bread is converted into the body and of the wine into the blood, "which conversion the Catholic Church most aptly calls transubstantiation," anathematising those who denied it. The Lateran definition of 1215 was thereby entrenched as settled Roman Catholic dogma.',
