        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '1928-01-01',
        datePrecision: 'YEAR',
        reason: 'Although condemned as heresy in 1327 and later placed on the Index of Forbidden Books, the Defensor Pacis was repeatedly put to use against papal claims — an English translation was printed in 1535 to support Henry VIII\'s break with Rome — and modern scholarship, from C. W. Previté-Orton\'s critical edition (1928) onward, treats it as a seminal work of secular political theory anticipating popular sovereignty and the separation of spiritual and temporal authority. The medieval heresy verdict thus no longer represents the settled estimate of the treatise, whose significance is now read very differently.',
