        reason: 'The nature of al-Sufi\'s "little cloud" remained open for centuries — catalogued among the nebulae and long disputed in the "Great Debate" over whether spiral nebulae lay inside or outside the Milky Way. In 1925 Edwin Hubble announced Cepheid variable stars in the Andromeda Nebula, fixing its distance far beyond the Galaxy and establishing it as a separate galaxy (M31). The object al-Sufi had logged in 964 was thereby settled as the nearest large external galaxy, retrospectively confirming his note as the first written sighting of a galaxy.',
