        reason: 'In Books V–VI of his commentary on the Almagest — the only books of that commentary to survive in Greek — Pappus calculates "the place and time of conjunction which gave rise to the eclipse in Tybi in 1068 after Nabonassar," which works out to 18 October 320 CE, and he writes as though describing an eclipse he had lately observed. It is a worked example of Ptolemy\'s parallax-based method for predicting solar eclipses, and the dated calculation is the firmest fixed point for when Pappus was active.',
