        community: 'INSTITUTIONAL',
        occurredAt: '0168-04-04',
        datePrecision: 'MONTH',
        reason: 'Li Xi was interred at Mawangdui with a lacquer-box library of silk manuscripts; a wooden inventory and "announcement to the underworld" tablet found in the tomb dates the burial to a day in the second lunar month of the twelfth year of Emperor Wen, fixing the manuscripts\' terminus ante quem to 168 BCE. Among them the Wushi\'er Bingfang and the medical and daoyin-exercise charts are the earliest surviving Chinese medical texts, while the astronomical silks — the Wuxing zhan (Prognostics of the Five Planets) and a divination chart of comets and clouds — preserve dated planetary observations and early depictions of comet forms.',
