        reason: 'After the naval victory over Sextus Pompeius, Octavian raised a temple to Apollo on the Palatine adjoining his own house and dedicated it on 9 October 28 BCE, a day already associated with victory cults. The complex included a portico with twin libraries of Greek and Latin works, among the largest collections in Rome. The poet Propertius, present at the opening, celebrated it in Elegy 2.31; Augustus records building the temple with its porticoes and library in his Res Gestae (19), and Cassius Dio (53.1.3) narrates its dedication.',
