        reason: 'On the authority of a senatus consultum and the Sibylline books, the quindecimviri sacris faciundis — headed by Augustus and Agrippa — conduct three successive nights of sacrifice at the Tarentum altar in the Campus Martius and three days of sacrifice on the Capitol and Palatine, from the night of 31 May to 3 June 17 BCE. On the final day twenty-seven boys and twenty-seven girls, all with both parents living, sing the Carmen Saeculare composed for the occasion by Quintus Horatius Flaccus. The Senate orders the entire proceedings inscribed on a marble (and bronze) stele.',
