    externalId: 'trajectory:siege-of-baideng-200bce',
    text: 'In the winter of 200 BCE the Xiongnu chanyu Modu (Maodun) lured the army of the Han founder Emperor Gaozu (Liu Bang) into a feigned retreat and encircled him for seven days on Mount Baideng near Pingcheng (modern Datong, Shanxi); the trapped emperor escaped only by negotiation, and the humiliation led the Han to adopt the heqin ("peace-and-kinship") policy of marriage alliance and tribute that governed Han–Xiongnu relations for the next sixty years.',
