        reason: 'On the coastal plain near Raphia, south of Gaza, the two largest armies the Hellenistic kingdoms ever fielded against one another — each tens of thousands strong with war elephants, the Ptolemaic African and the Seleucid Indian — clash; Antiochus\'s right wins, but Ptolemy rallies his phalanx, which includes 20,000 newly trained native Egyptians, and breaks the Seleucid centre. Antiochus flees and cedes Coele-Syria. The battle was commemorated within months by the trilingual Raphia Decree, set up by an assembly of Egyptian priests at Memphis to thank Ptolemy for the victory.',
