        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '1974-01-01',
        datePrecision: 'YEAR',
        reason: 'G. J. Toomer, reconstructing the calculation in 1974, identified the eclipse as that of 14 March 190 BCE — total along the path through the Hellespont and about four-fifths at Alexandria, matching Pappus\'s figures, the geometry, and the historical setting (the eclipse was total at Nicaea, Hipparchus\'s birthplace). The identification is the standard one in the history-of-astronomy literature, and Hipparchus\'s two-station parallax method is recognised as the first observational determination of the Earth–Moon distance.',
