        reason: 'Funan, the first major state of mainland Southeast Asia, ran an entrepôt at Óc Eo on the lower Mekong where merchants from the Roman world, India, and Han China exchanged goods; the brick foundations, canals, glass beads, Indian Brahmanical statuary, Chinese bronze mirrors, and Roman gold medallions buried at the site form a material primary record of that trade. Mid-3rd-century Chinese envoys of the Wu kingdom (Kang Tai and Zhu Ying) also left near-contemporaneous written accounts of Funan, but the Funanese themselves recorded no surviving day-dated chronicle, and the buried city was forgotten for over a millennium.',
