        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '2008-07-29',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Jaime Gongora and colleagues, re-examining the mitochondrial sequences in PNAS, argue that the El Arenal haplotype is in fact widespread in Indo-European and Southeast Asian chickens and is consistent with a European introduction, and that the original analysis may have been affected by modern DNA contamination; a 2014 ancient-DNA study reaches the same contamination conclusion. The Storey team replies in defence of a pre-Columbian Polynesian age, and the question of whether Polynesians delivered chickens to South America before Columbus remains genuinely unresolved — the radiocarbon dates undisputed, the Polynesian attribution contested.',
