        reason: 'Pablo Muñoz-Rodríguez and colleagues, publishing a genome-scale phylogeny of Ipomoea in Current Biology, find that sweet potato\'s wild relatives and estimated divergence times place its split from American lineages well over 100,000 years ago — long before any human presence in Polynesia — and argue the crop could have reached the Pacific by natural long-distance dispersal of seeds, without a human carrier. The dating of the crop\'s pre-European presence in Polynesia is not in dispute, but whether Polynesian voyagers themselves brought it from South America is again openly contested between the human-contact and natural-dispersal models.',
