        toAxis: 'RECORDED',
        community: 'PUBLIC',
        occurredAt: '1533-07-26',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Although Atahualpa had filled a room with gold and twice with silver as ransom, the Spaniards, fearing an Inca army was assembling, tried him on charges including the killing of his half-brother Huáscar and idolatry, and condemned him to be burned alive. Persuaded that burning would destroy his body and bar his afterlife, Atahualpa accepted baptism, and the sentence was commuted to strangulation by garrote, carried out in the plaza of Cajamarca. Xerez and the other Cajamarca chroniclers recorded the execution.',
