        reason: 'Stirred by the 1511 Advent sermons of the Dominican friar Antonio de Montesinos denouncing the destruction of the Taíno, a junta of theologians and jurists convened at Burgos drafted the first written code regulating Spanish treatment of the Indians. Promulgated by Ferdinand II on 27 December 1512, the thirty-five Leyes de Burgos ordered that Indians be instructed in the faith, set limits on labour drafts in the mines, required food, wages and rest, forbade beating or branding the natives, and barred work for pregnant women — a legal recognition, however unenforced, that the Indigenous people were free subjects of the crown rather than chattel. The text of the laws is the contemporaneous primary record.',
