        reason: 'With Charles IV and Ferdinand VII lured to Bayonne and the French army of Joachim Murat occupying the capital, a crowd gathered before the Royal Palace on the morning of 2 May 1808 to stop the removal of the Infante Francisco de Paula. Murat sent Imperial Guard cavalry and artillery, which fired on the crowd; street fighting spread to the Puerta del Sol, and the artillery officers Luis Daoíz and Pedro Velarde made the only organised military stand at the Monteleón barracks before being killed. That night and through 3 May, French firing squads executed hundreds of captured Madrileños — the scene Francisco de Goya painted in The Third of May 1808. The events are documented in Murat\'s own proclamations, Spanish chronicles, and contemporaneous accounts.',
