        occurredAt: '1885-07-06',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Pasteur had developed a rabies vaccine by drying the spinal cords of infected rabbits to attenuate the agent, but had not tried it on a human. On 6 July 1885 the mother of Joseph Meister, bitten fourteen times two days earlier by a rabid dog, brought the boy to Pasteur. Over ten days Pasteur (the injections given by physicians, as he held no medical licence) administered a graded series of increasingly virulent cord preparations. Meister did not develop rabies and recovered fully.',
