        reason: 'Until 2019 no black hole had ever been directly imaged; their existence rested on indirect evidence such as orbiting stars and accretion-disc radiation. Using a planet-scale array of radio telescopes linked by very-long-baseline interferometry to observe the galaxy M87 in April 2017, the Event Horizon Telescope collaboration reconstructed an image of the central black hole. At six simultaneous press conferences on 10 April 2019 and in six papers in The Astrophysical Journal Letters, they presented a ~42-microarcsecond ring of emission around a central shadow about 2.6 times the Schwarzschild radius, consistent with a ~6.5-billion-solar-mass black hole. The result was placed on the scientific record as a dated, peer-reviewed publication.',
