        occurredAt: '1664-11-07',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'The comet was first reported from Spain on 7 November 1664 and passed perihelion around 24–25 November; Hevelius recorded its long curving tail on 21 December. It was observed independently across the continents — Cassini, Hooke, Newton and Hevelius in Europe, Samuel Pepys and John Evelyn in London, and astronomers in China and Japan — and in New England the minister Samuel Danforth published An Astronomical Description of the Late Comet or Blazing Star (Cambridge, Massachusetts, 1665), one of the first works of astronomy printed in America. The dispersed, contemporaneous notices fix the apparition to the winter of 1664–65.',
