        datePrecision: 'MONTH',
        reason: 'Italos, holder of the chair of "consul of the philosophers," had been examined once before and acquitted in 1076/77; after the accession of Alexios I he was retried and in March 1082 found guilty of heterodoxy. A synod condemned a list of propositions drawn from his teaching — that matter and ideas are eternal, that souls pre-exist, and that the methods of pagan philosophy could adjudicate Christian doctrine — and the anathemas were incorporated into the Synodikon of Orthodoxy. The trial is documented in the synodal acts, the Synodikon, and Anna Komnene\'s Alexiad.',
