        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '1946-01-01',
        datePrecision: 'YEAR',
        reason: 'The result is widely doubted. The prevailing "tetranucleotide hypothesis" held that DNA was a monotonous, simple molecule incapable of storing the diversity of genetic information, which most biologists believed must reside in proteins. Critics, notably Alfred Mirsky at Rockefeller, argue that trace protein contaminating the DNA preparations could be the true transforming agent. The genetic role of DNA remains contested.',
