        reason: 'With Emperor Michael III and his army away campaigning against the Abbasids in Asia Minor, a Rus\' fleet entered the Bosphorus at sunset on 18 June 860, pillaging the suburbs and monasteries of the capital and the Princes\' Islands. Patriarch Photios, present in the city, preached two homilies during and after the raid — describing the attackers who fell upon the city "as a swarm of wasps" — the first time the Rus\' appear in a contemporary Greek source. The siege was lifted and the Rus\' withdrew by early August, and Photios delivered a thanksgiving sermon on the city\'s deliverance.',
