        reason: 'Having abandoned an attempt on Constantinople, Leo of Tripoli turned his fleet of some fifty ships against the poorly prepared Thessalonica and broke into the city from the sea on 29 July 904 after a brief assault on the walls. The raiders plundered the city for a week, freed about 4,000 Muslim prisoners, and seized roughly 22,000 inhabitants for the slave markets before sailing back to the Levant. John Kaminiates, a cleric carried off in the sack and later ransomed, set down a circumstantial first-person narrative of the fall — one of the fullest eyewitness accounts of any medieval Byzantine catastrophe.',
