        toAxis: 'SETTLED',
        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '2000-01-01',
        datePrecision: 'YEAR',
        reason: 'The fall of Thessalonica in late July 904 is corroborated by the Byzantine chronicle tradition (the continuators of Theophanes, Symeon the Logothete) and reflected in Arabic notices, and is treated as a settled event of the Byzantine–Arab naval wars. Scholarship has debated the authenticity of Kaminiates\' account and reconciled it with al-Tabari\'s divergent report that Leo struck Attaleia, but the sack itself, its approximate date, and the scale of the captivity are accepted; the episode prompted the strengthening of the city\'s sea walls.',
