        reason: 'In 1984 the fossil-hunter Kamoya Kimeu spotted a fragment of skull eroding from the bank of the Nariokotome River, west of Lake Turkana in Kenya; excavation recovered most of the skeleton of an adolescent male. Brown, Harris, Leakey and Walker describe it as KNM-WT 15000 in "Early Homo erectus skeleton from west Lake Turkana, Kenya" in Nature on 29 August 1985 — a roughly 1.6-million-year-old Homo erectus and the most complete early-hominin skeleton recovered up to that time.',
