        reason: 'A second, enlarged edition of 2,000 copies followed in September 1822, and the mission press went on to print hymnals, scripture, and schoolbooks that drove Hawaiian literacy to among the highest rates in the nineteenth-century world. Surviving imprints in the Hawaiian Imprint collection at the Library of Congress and elsewhere fix the 7 January 1822 pīʻāpā as the first printing in the Hawaiian language beyond dispute.',
