        toAxis: 'RECORDED',
        community: 'PUBLIC',
        occurredAt: '1819-10-01',
        datePrecision: 'MONTH',
        reason: 'After the death of Kamehameha I in May 1819, the powerful chiefesses Kaʻahumanu and Keōpūolani pressed the new king Liholiho to abolish the ʻai kapu — the system of sacred prohibitions that, among much else, forbade men and women from eating together and barred women from foods such as pork and bananas. Following two days of hesitation aboard a canoe, Liholiho came ashore and sat down to eat with the women at a feast prepared by the chiefesses; messengers were then sent across the islands announcing that "eating was free" and the kapu had fallen. The high priest Hewahewa ordered the burning of the temple images. The breaking of the taboo and the collapse of the old order were witnessed by the assembled chiefs and recorded by Hawaiian informants and the missionaries who arrived months later.',
