        occurredAt: '1992-01-01',
        datePrecision: 'YEAR',
        reason: 'Resistance led by Liholiho\'s cousin Kekuaokalani was crushed at the Battle of Kuamoʻo in December 1819, and when the first American Board missionaries reached Hawaiʻi in 1820 they found the traditional religion already overthrown. Historians of Hawaiʻi — drawing on the early accounts of David Malo, Samuel Kamakau and the missionaries — treat the 1819 ʻAi Noa as a settled turning point at which the kapu system and the state cult were formally abolished by the Hawaiian rulers themselves, even as the precise motives of Kaʻahumanu and Liholiho continue to be analysed.',
