        toAxis: 'RECORDED',
        community: 'PUBLIC',
        occurredAt: '1845-03-11',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Hōne Heke, a Ngāpuhi rangatira who had been the first to sign the Treaty of Waitangi, came to see the British flagstaff above Kororāreka as the symbol of a sovereignty and customs regime that had stripped his people of trade revenue; he had cut it down three times since July 1844. On the morning of 11 March 1845 a coordinated assault by Heke\'s and Kawiti\'s roughly 600 warriors drew off the garrison of about 140 from HMS Hazard and the 96th Regiment, Heke felled the flagstaff a fourth time, and an explosion in the powder magazine forced the British to abandon and evacuate the town, which was then looted and burned. The battle was reported at once in dispatches and the colonial press.',
