        community: 'JUDICIAL',
        occurredAt: '1996-12-23',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Ruling on claims by the Wik and Thayorre peoples over land on Cape York Peninsula, the High Court holds 4–3 that the statutory pastoral leases in question did not confer exclusive possession and therefore did not necessarily extinguish native title. Native title rights and pastoral lease rights can coexist; where the two are inconsistent, the rights of the pastoralist prevail. The decision answers the question left open by Mabo (1992) — whether native title survives on the roughly 40% of Australia held under pastoral lease — and is entered into the law reports as binding precedent.',
