        community: 'JUDICIAL',
        occurredAt: '2014-06-26',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'In a unanimous decision written by Chief Justice Beverley McLachlin, the Supreme Court of Canada allows the Tsilhqot\'in appeal and issues the first declaration of Aboriginal title in Canadian history over a defined territory. The Court holds that Aboriginal title confers the right to decide how the land is used and to occupy, possess, manage and benefit from it, and that the Crown cannot proceed with development (such as clearcut logging) on title land without the consent of the title-holders or, failing that, without meeting a stringent justification test. The long-open question of whether and where Aboriginal title could be proven as more than a site-specific right is answered and entered into the law reports.',
