        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'In a unanimous judgment by Chief Justice Brian Dickson and Justice Gérard La Forest, the Supreme Court of Canada hands down its first substantive ruling on section 35(1) of the Constitution Act 1982. Ronald Edward Sparrow, a member of the Musqueam Band, had been charged with fishing with a drift net longer than his band\'s licence permitted and defended himself by asserting an Aboriginal right to fish. The Court holds that Aboriginal rights existing in 1982 are "recognized and affirmed" by s.35(1), that they are not absolute but may be infringed only where the Crown demonstrates a valid legislative objective (such as conservation) consistent with its fiduciary duty to Indigenous peoples — establishing the "Sparrow test" for justified infringement, which is entered into the law reports as the foundational framework for Aboriginal rights in Canada.',
