        community: 'JUDICIAL',
        occurredAt: '2003-06-19',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Ruling on a claim by eight iwi of the Marlborough Sounds, the Court of Appeal unanimously holds that the Māori Land Court has jurisdiction to determine whether the foreshore and seabed are Māori customary land. Chief Justice Sian Elias reasons that the transfer of sovereignty did not of itself affect customary property, which survives at common law until lawfully extinguished, and that the contrary 1963 decision In re the Ninety-Mile Beach — which had held that customary title to the foreshore was automatically extinguished once the adjoining dry land lost its customary status — was "wrong in law" and is overruled. The judgment provokes immediate political controversy; Parliament responds with the Foreshore and Seabed Act 2004 vesting the foreshore and seabed in the Crown, but that Act is itself repealed and replaced by the Marine and Coastal Area (Takutai Moana) Act 2011, and the Court\'s holding that customary interests were not automatically extinguished stands as the settled legal position.',
