        reason: 'Alan Thorne and colleagues report in the Journal of Human Evolution that the Lake Mungo 3 skeleton — the oldest known ritual ochre burial and a key fossil of early Australians — is about 62,000 ± 6,000 years old, combining uranium-series, electron-spin-resonance and optically-stimulated-luminescence dating. The figure pushes the human occupation of Australia far earlier than the archaeological consensus and is immediately contested, not least because the proposed age is older than the sediment layer in which the body was buried.',
