        community: 'PUBLIC',
        occurredAt: '1688-06-05',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'As King Narai lay dying, his foster brother and Master of the Royal Elephants, Phetracha, seized power in mid-May 1688, arresting Phaulkon — the Greek adventurer who had directed Narai\'s alliance with France — on 18 May. After interrogation Phaulkon was executed at Lopburi on 5 June 1688; Narai\'s heirs were put to death and the king himself died on 10–11 July. Phetracha was crowned on 1 August and besieged the French troops at Bangkok until General Desfarges agreed to evacuate on 13 November 1688. The events were recorded by French eyewitnesses, notably the engineer Jean Vollant des Verquains and the Jesuit Guy Tachard.',
