        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '1990-01-01',
        datePrecision: 'YEAR',
        reason: 'The occurrence, dates, and devastating scale of the fire are uncontested, but its cause has been argued since 1812 — Napoleon blamed Russian incendiarism, and most historians attribute the initial fires to the scorched-earth strategy associated with the city\'s governor Count Fyodor Rostopchin (fire engines having been removed and a captured policeman admitting to arson orders), while a minority view, voiced by Tolstoy, holds the fires arose accidentally in an abandoned wooden city full of unsupervised troops. Modern scholarship treats deliberate Russian destruction as the principal cause while acknowledging the surviving uncertainty over orders and intent.',
