        reason: 'A new star blazes up in the southern constellation Lupus, so bright — by modern estimate apparent magnitude near −7.5, brighter than any other stellar event in recorded history — that contemporaries report objects could be seen on the ground at night. It is logged independently across the Northern Hemisphere within weeks: the Song dynasty astronomical bureau in China (recording 1 May 1006), Japanese observers, the Egyptian physician Ali ibn Ridwan, the Persian Ibn Sina, and the monks of the Abbey of St Gallen in Switzerland; first sightings from Yemen may reach back to mid-to-late April. A petroglyph by the Hohokam in the White Tank Mountains of Arizona, showing a star-like object above a scorpion, has been proposed as an Indigenous North American record of the same event.',
