        reason: 'Ramanujan, then a shipping-office clerk in Madras with no formal mathematical training, mailed Hardy a letter dated 16 January 1913 containing some 120 theorems on infinite series, continued fractions, and number theory. Hardy, after first suspecting a fraud, examined the formulae with Littlewood and concluded they "must be true, because, if they were not true, no one would have had the imagination to invent them," judging Ramanujan a mathematician "of the highest quality." The correspondence is the founding record of Ramanujan\'s recognition.',
