        occurredAt: '1960-09-19',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'After nine years of negotiations begun in 1951 and brokered by the World Bank — whose president Eugene R. Black insisted the dispute be treated as an engineering problem rather than a political one — Nehru, Ayub Khan, and the Bank\'s representative W. A. B. Iliff signed the Indus Waters Treaty at Karachi on 19 September 1960. The treaty gave India unrestricted use of the three eastern rivers and Pakistan the three western rivers, with a transition period and Bank-financed link canals and storage works to replace Pakistan\'s lost eastern supplies. The signing was reported internationally and registered as a binding international agreement.',
