        community: 'PUBLIC',
        occurredAt: '1584-07-10',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Having gained access to William\'s household by posing as a French Protestant, Balthasar Gérard waits as the prince leaves the dining hall of the Prinsenhof and shoots him in the chest at close range with a pistol; William dies almost immediately. Gérard is captured, tried, and put to death by prolonged torture and quartering. The killing — carried out under the terms of Philip II\'s 1580 ban offering 25,000 crowns for William\'s death — is reported across Europe within days and recorded in Dutch and foreign accounts and printed news sheets.',
