        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '2006-10-16',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'A U.S. WC-135 atmospheric-sampling aircraft and ground monitoring stations detected radioactive xenon isotopes — produced only by the fission that occurs in a nuclear explosion and able to seep out even from underground tests — vented from the site; a station in northern Canada picked up the xenon traces, and on 16 October 2006 U.S. intelligence confirmed that the explosion had been nuclear in nature. The radionuclide evidence settled the physical question, establishing the 9 October 2006 event as North Korea\'s first confirmed nuclear detonation.',
