        toAxis: 'RECORDED',
        community: 'PUBLIC',
        occurredAt: '1970-12-17',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'After the government announced steep price increases on food and basic goods on 12 December, workers at the Lenin Shipyard in Gdańsk and other Baltic-coast plants struck and took to the streets from 14 December. On 17 December soldiers under orders to stop workers returning to work fired into the crowd emerging from trains at Gdynia station, killing at least eleven there; in total at least forty-four people died and over a thousand were wounded. The killings were witnessed by thousands and immediately known across the coastal cities, though the regime suppressed casualty figures and buried victims at night.',
