        occurredAt: '1066-04-01',
        datePrecision: 'MONTH',
        reason: 'The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that in 1066 "a sign such as men never saw before was seen in the heavens" — a long-haired star that appeared after Easter and shone for a week. The monk Eilmer of Malmesbury, recalling the comet of his youth, is said to have cried "You have come, have you? ... you are much more terrible now," reading it as a portent of disaster. The same apparition is depicted on the Bayeux Tapestry above a fearful Harold, taken as an omen of the Norman Conquest later that year.',
