        reason: 'As the plague spread and rumours that Jews had poisoned wells and springs fuelled pogroms across the Rhineland and beyond, Clement VI, from Avignon, issued the bull Quamvis Perfidiam on 26 September 1348 — following a first protective bull of 6 July 1348. He declared that those who blamed the Jews had been "seduced by that liar, the Devil," took the Jews under papal protection, and argued from observation that the accusation was false: the same plague, "by the hidden judgment of God," afflicted the Jews themselves and many peoples who had never lived alongside them, and raged in places where no Jews dwelt. The bulls survive in the papal records, fixing the act and its date.',
