    text: 'In a paper published in Nature on 14 November 1985 (vol. 318, pp. 162–163), Harold Kroto, James Heath, Sean O\'Brien, Robert Curl, and Richard Smalley of Rice University reported that laser vaporisation of graphite produces an exceptionally stable 60-carbon cluster (C60), which they proposed has the cage-like structure of a truncated icosahedron — a hollow soccer-ball molecule they named buckminsterfullerene — establishing a previously unknown third allotropic form of carbon beyond graphite and diamond.',
