    text: 'On 16 December 1689 William III and Mary II gave royal assent to the Bill of Rights, which enacted the Declaration of Right of the Convention Parliament — declaring illegal the suspending and dispensing of laws by royal authority, prohibiting taxation and a standing army in peacetime without parliamentary consent, guaranteeing free elections, freedom of speech in Parliament, and the right to petition, and barring excessive bail and cruel and unusual punishment — settling the constitutional supremacy of Parliament over the Crown after the Glorious Revolution.',
