        reason: 'From 29 September 1998 a severe outbreak of febrile encephalitis struck pig-farming communities in Perak, Negeri Sembilan and Selangor, Malaysia. Malaysian authorities initially attributed the deaths to Japanese encephalitis (JE) — a mosquito-borne flavivirus — and launched mosquito-control and JE-vaccination campaigns. But the epidemiology was anomalous: cases clustered among adult male pig handlers rather than children, many victims had been JE-vaccinated, and the disease tracked direct contact with sick pigs, putting the JE attribution in dispute.',
