        fromAxis: 'RECORDED',
        toAxis: 'SETTLED',
        community: 'INSTITUTIONAL',
        occurredAt: '1948-10-29',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'DDT was deployed at scale during the Second World War to control typhus- and malaria-carrying insects — most famously halting a typhus outbreak in Naples in 1943–44 — establishing it as the dominant insecticide of the era. The Karolinska Institute awarded Müller the 1948 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine "for his discovery of the high efficiency of DDT as a contact poison against several arthropods," ratifying the discovery\'s significance.',
