        reason: 'A team led by Zahi Hawass and Yehia Z. Gad applied microsatellite-based ancient-DNA fingerprinting to 16 mummies of the late 18th Dynasty (the Egyptian Mummy Project), with analytical steps independently replicated in a second ancient-DNA laboratory. The study, published in JAMA on 17 February 2010, reconstructed a five-generation pedigree placing Tutankhamun as the son of the KV55 male (Akhenaten) and the KV35 "Younger Lady," and detected malaria-parasite DNA together with evidence of bone pathology.',
