        reason: 'After dissecting the head of a great white shark sent to him at Florence in October 1666, Steno recognised that the "tongue stones" (glossopetrae) found in rock were identical to shark teeth and must therefore be organic remains buried in sediment. Generalising the problem of one solid lying within another, he set out in the Prodromus (1669) the principles that layers of sediment are deposited horizontally and in sequence, with lower strata older than those above — the founding statements of stratigraphy.',
