        occurredAt: '1937-07-02',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'After departing Lae, New Guinea, for tiny Howland Island, Earhart\'s Lockheed Electra was tracked by radio aboard the cutter Itasca, stationed off Howland to guide her in. Her signals grew stronger through the morning of 2 July 1937 — "we must be on you but cannot see you... gas is running low" — before a final transmission at about 08:43 local time placing the plane on a line of position; she was never heard from again. The Itasca radio logs are the contemporaneous primary record of the disappearance.',
