        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '1953-09-04',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Recording eye movements and brain activity through the night, Aserinsky (then a graduate student) and his advisor Kleitman observed that distinct periods of rapid, jerky eye motion recurred at regular intervals during sleep, accompanied by a low-voltage EEG pattern and a high incidence of dream recall when subjects were woken. Their report, "Regularly Occurring Periods of Eye Motility, and Concomitant Phenomena, During Sleep" (Science 118:273–274, 4 September 1953), overturned the prevailing view of sleep as a single passive state and founded the modern science of sleep stages.',
