        occurredAt: '2017-06-07',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Re-excavation of the Jebel Irhoud site near Safi, Morocco, recovered fossils of at least five individuals — skull, jaw, teeth and limb bones — with facial features essentially modern though the braincase remained archaic. Thermoluminescence dating of flint artefacts burnt in the same layers returned an age of about 315,000 years. Two papers — Hublin et al. and Richter et al. — published in Nature on 7 June 2017 reported the finds, making Jebel Irhoud the earliest known site yielding Homo sapiens and overturning the prevailing model that anatomically modern humans first appeared in East Africa about 200,000 years ago.',
