        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '1749-01-01',
        datePrecision: 'YEAR',
        reason: 'al-Battani\'s dated eclipse observations became fixed benchmarks in the history of astronomy. Edmond Halley used al-Battani\'s eclipse and equinox data to derive lunar positions for the years 881–901, and in 1749 Richard Dunthorne used the same medieval observations to assign a numerical value to the apparent acceleration of the Moon\'s mean motion (about 10 arcseconds per century). The 901 eclipses were independently confirmed by modern computation — the total solar eclipse of 23 January 901 and the total lunar eclipse of 3 August 901 both appear in NASA\'s Five Millennium catalogues — and they remain part of the evidence for the long-term slowing of Earth\'s rotation.',
