        reason: 'While ship\'s surgeon on HMS Salisbury, Lind selected twelve patients "as similar as I could have them" in the severity of their scurvy, lodged them together, and fed them the same basic diet, varying only the daily supplement among six pairs: cider, dilute sulphuric acid (elixir of vitriol), vinegar, seawater, a medicinal electuary, and two oranges with one lemon. The citrus pair improved so dramatically that one was fit for duty within six days. The trial — with its use of contemporaneous control groups — is recorded by Lind himself in his "Treatise of the Scurvy," a contemporaneous primary account.',
