        reason: 'Nitrous oxide anaesthesia, abandoned for surgery after Wells\'s failure, was revived by Gardner Quincy Colton and became standard in dentistry from the 1860s and remains in use today; Wells\'s priority was recognised when the Paris Medical Society honoured him as the discoverer of anaesthesia shortly before his death in 1848, and the American Dental Association and American Medical Association later formally credited him with the discovery. That inhaled agents can safely abolish surgical and dental pain is settled medicine.',
