        reason: 'After liquefying air and allowing it to evaporate, Ramsay and Travers collected the gases boiling off in order of volatility, removing nitrogen, oxygen and argon. Having found krypton in May 1898, in June 1898 they examined a lighter fraction whose discharge tube blazed with a distinctive crimson-orange light unlike any known element\'s spectrum; Travers wrote that "the blaze of crimson light from the tube told its own story." Ramsay\'s son suggested the name neon (Greek for "new"), and the element was reported to the scientific community as a new member of Ramsay\'s family of inert gases.',
