        occurredAt: '1820-09-18',
        datePrecision: 'MONTH',
        reason: 'After François Arago reported Ørsted\'s deflection of a compass needle by a current to the Académie des sciences on 4 September 1820 and demonstrated it on 11 September, Ampère repeated the experiment and extended it within days. In successive memoirs read to the Académie over the following sessions of September and October 1820, he reported that two parallel current-carrying conductors attract when their currents are parallel and repel when antiparallel — a force between currents themselves, not merely between currents and magnets — and began casting it as a mathematical law. He coined the term "electrodynamics" for the new field and devised an early galvanometer to measure the effect.',
