        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '1785-06-01',
        datePrecision: 'MONTH',
        reason: 'Coulomb, a military engineer, built a sensitive torsion balance — a charged metal-coated ball on an insulating rod suspended by a fine silver wire whose twist measured tiny forces — and used it to measure the repulsion between like charges as their separation varied. His "Premier mémoire sur l\'électricité et le magnétisme," read to the Académie Royale des Sciences in 1785 and printed in its Histoire (pp. 569–577), reported that the electric force falls off as the inverse square of the distance, mirroring Newtonian gravitation. A second memoir extended the result to magnetic poles and to the product of the charges.',
