        toAxis: 'SETTLED',
        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '2014-10-23',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Because the Neanderthal admixture blocks in the Ust\'-Ishim genome had not yet been broken up by many generations of recombination, they constrained the date of Neanderthal–modern human interbreeding to about 52,000–58,000 years ago, and the genome anchored a slow human germline mutation rate (~0.4–0.5 × 10⁻⁹ per base pair per year). These values became widely adopted reference points in human evolutionary genetics, settling Ust\'-Ishim as a calibration benchmark for the modern-human timeline.',
