        toAxis: 'RECORDED',
        community: 'EXPERT_LITERATURE',
        occurredAt: '0968-12-22',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Leo the Deacon (c. 950–994), an eyewitness in Constantinople, set down a strikingly precise account of the total eclipse that darkened the city about the fourth hour of the day on 22 December 968: the sky went dark, the bright stars appeared, and a faint ring of light encircled the obscured solar disk. While the corona is visible at every total eclipse, his is the first written description detailed enough to be recognised unambiguously as the corona — making it the earliest such record in the history of solar physics.',
