    text: 'The teachings of the Oxford theologian John Wycliffe — including his denial of transubstantiation and his attacks on clerical wealth and authority — were condemned as heretical and erroneous by the Church. Pope Gregory XI issued five bulls against Wycliffe on 22 May 1377 censuring eighteen propositions from his De civili dominio, and the Blackfriars Synod (the "Earthquake Synod") at London on 21 May 1382 condemned twenty-four of his propositions, ten as heretical (chiefly on the Eucharist) and fourteen as erroneous. The Council of Constance confirmed the condemnation on 4 May 1415, declaring Wycliffe a heretic and ordering his writings burned and his remains exhumed.',
