    externalId: 'trajectory:gell-mann-zweig-quark-model-1964',
    text: 'In early 1964 Murray Gell-Mann and, independently, George Zweig proposed that the proliferating "elementary" particles known as hadrons are in fact built from a small number of more fundamental constituents — which Gell-Mann named "quarks" — carrying fractional electric charges. The scheme economically organised the known baryons and mesons and predicted their properties, but fractionally charged particles had never been observed and many physicists initially treated quarks as a mathematical bookkeeping device rather than physical objects. Deep inelastic electron-scattering experiments at the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center in 1968–1969 revealed hard point-like constituents inside protons, providing the first experimental evidence that quarks are real and converting the model from a classification trick into established physics.',
