        occurredAt: '2021-10-07',
        datePrecision: 'DAY',
        reason: 'Before Chang\'e-5, the youngest precisely dated lunar basalts were about 2.9–3.2 billion years old and the duration of lunar volcanism was inferred only indirectly from crater counts. On 7 October 2021, Che et al. published "Age and composition of young basalts on the Moon, measured from samples returned by Chang\'e-5" in Science, reporting a lead–lead crystallisation age of about 2.03 billion years for the returned basalt clasts; a companion study in Nature reached the same ~2.0-billion-year result, recording for the first time direct radiometric evidence of young lunar volcanism.',
