    externalId: 'trajectory:marconi-wireless-patent-1896',
    text: 'On 2 June 1896, having moved to London the previous year, Guglielmo Marconi filed British Patent No. 12039, "Improvements in Transmitting Electrical impulses and Signals, and in Apparatus therefor" — the first patent for a communication system based on radio waves. Building on Hertz\'s demonstration that electromagnetic waves exist, Marconi\'s apparatus turned the laboratory effect into a practical system for signalling without wires over useful distances. Through 1896 and 1897 he demonstrated transmission over progressively greater ranges — across Salisbury Plain and over water in the Bristol Channel — and the patent was granted in 1897, the year he formed the Wireless Telegraph & Signal Company. Wireless telegraphy went on to become a routine technology, and Marconi shared the 1909 Nobel Prize in Physics for his contribution to its development.',
