    text: 'The Twenty-fourth Amendment to the United States Constitution prohibits the federal government and the states from conditioning the right to vote in federal elections on payment of a poll tax or any other tax. Poll taxes, used chiefly in Southern states, had functioned since the post-Reconstruction era as a barrier to voting by poor citizens and especially Black citizens. Congress proposed the amendment to the states on 27 August 1962 and submitted it on 24 September 1962; ratification was completed on 23 January 1964 when South Dakota became the thirty-eighth state to ratify, providing the required three-fourths majority. The amendment was thereby written permanently into the constitutional record; the Supreme Court extended the same principle to state and local elections two years later in Harper v. Virginia State Board of Elections (1966).',
