    text: 'The Act of Uniformity 1549 (2 & 3 Edward VI c. 1), which received royal assent on 14 March 1549, imposed on the Church of England a single uniform order of worship — the first Book of Common Prayer, compiled chiefly by Archbishop Thomas Cranmer — and made its use compulsory in every parish from Whitsunday, 9 June 1549, replacing the Latin missals and breviaries of the medieval rite with an English-language liturgy. The settlement was overturned during the Catholic restoration under Mary I, whose first Parliament repealed the Edwardian religious statutes in December 1553 and restored the Latin Mass; it was then re-established under Elizabeth I, whose Act of Uniformity (royal assent 8 May 1559) reinstated the Book of Common Prayer from 24 June 1559. English-language Prayer Book worship has remained the established liturgy of the Church of England ever since.',
