    externalId: 'trajectory:gabor-holography-invention-1948',
    text: 'Dennis Gabor, working at British Thomson-Houston, conceived the principle of holography in 1947 as a method to improve the resolution of the electron microscope by recording both the amplitude and the phase of a wavefront in a single interference pattern (a "hologram") that could later be reconstructed. He set out the principle in the paper "A new microscopic principle," published in Nature (vol. 161, pp. 777–778) on 15 May 1948. Lacking a coherent light source, holography remained a laboratory curiosity until the invention of the laser in 1960 made practical three-dimensional holograms possible; Gabor received the 1971 Nobel Prize in Physics for the invention.',
